{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:c9a93645b85735ba7e1f0be68ad8b5eaac27c26108266b6748803b452aa33a9a","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.26},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Spread 19/20/16/22/30. I move up, and the move is carried by ONE argument I judge a real hit, applied at PARTIAL strength.\n\nCONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE averages >= 105 over 2027-2036), MARGINAL 0.50. THE RED-TEAM HIT: MOVE is a NORMALIZED BASIS-POINT yield-volatility index, so for a given PROPORTIONAL rate vol it scales with the YIELD LEVEL. The decade averages all three Wave-1 seats anchored on (~75 in 2010-19, ~75-85 in 2013-22, \"roughly 85 to 100\" long-run per informal analyst sources — caveat preserved, no official ICE per-decade table was located by any seat) were generated in a 1.5-2.5% nominal 10y world. The locked starting point is a 4-5% nominal 10y with a 2.60% real yield. Two seats WROTE the sentence and then failed to price it: the Fermi seat wrote \"MOVE is normalized bp vol, so with G7 10y nominals at 4-5% its floor is structurally above the ZIRP era\" and set 0.45; the Scout wrote the same thing and set 0.32 on the explicit ground that \">= 105 is above every decade average on record except 1987-94 and 2022-24\" — a comparison across incommensurable rate regimes. That is the Scout's weakest number and I down-weight it hard. BUT THE RED-TEAMER OVERSHOOTS at 0.62, and the empirical check he skipped is decisive: the correct analogue decade is 1998-2007, when 10y nominals ran 4-5% and MOVE averaged ~90-100 — NOT 105+. The scaling is real but SUB-LINEAR, because much of rate vol is policy-uncertainty vol expressed in bp rather than proportionally. Re-leveling his calm state to ~90-95 rather than 95-100 puts the required stress share of the decade at ~0.25-0.35 rather than his 0.15-0.25 or the Fermi 0.50. Supports for the upper half: my own CQ4 posterior of 2.2% real implies G7 10y nominals ~4.4-4.9% across the window, and my CQ4 P90 of 3.4% is by construction a high-MOVE term-premium-repricing world; plus duration supply at >100% debt/GDP, net interest to ~3% of GDP, eroding convenience yields, and basis-trade plumbing (commentary describes \"typical gross leverage in the 20-50x range\" such that a 1% drop in Treasury prices translates into a 20-50% equity hit — commentary, NOT Fed/OFR/ICE primary data, and no source confirms a RECORD in basis-trade leverage; a Fed-linked analysis cited secondhand notes TIC data \"likely understate the amount of U.S. government debt held through offshore financial centers\"). Against: the structural vol-selling bid that compressed MOVE through 2025-26, and a decade average that must absorb several likely-calm early years. The Scout's current-level search is genuinely UNRESOLVED and poorly sourced (70.9 on 17 Jul 2026 via Yahoo Finance, a third-party aggregator never checked against ICE; 103.01 on 26 Mar 2026 from a dated commentary piece; ~75.4 against \"above 170 during the April 2025 hedge-fund basis-trade stress\"; an undated Convex page at 66 — these cannot all be current), so I discount it rather than let it drive. A = 0.50.\n\nCONJUNCT B. B1 (at least one MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough in 2027-36) = 0.80. Hazard build: ~8 episodes in 55 years (1973-74, 1980-82, 1987, 1990, 2000-02, 2007-09, 2020, 2022 at roughly -25/-27% USD), ~0.14/yr, so 0.76-0.80 over ten years. Lifted by the concentration structure (the AI complex at ~35-40% of MSCI World cap against ~$1.4T of circular vendor/cloud/chip commitments backed by only ~$300B cumulative equity and ~$650B debt issuance — a 50% derating of a 38% weight is -19% on the index before any contagion, and the locked GFSR stress case is itself a 20% AI-led equity shock), then trimmed back by the observed 2026 counterexample: a Morgan Stanley technology-momentum AI proxy fell 19% through the quarter and \"as high as 37% from its June peak to the July trough\" while MSCI ACWI GAINED 1.2% over three months, which is direct evidence the complex can break WITHOUT the index breaking. Net 0.80.\nB2 (conditional on a drawdown, G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominals FAIL to fall >= 50 bp, or rise >= 25 bp, in the window) = 0.42. All three Wave-1 seats landed independently at 0.375-0.38 off the same 3-of-8 split (1973-74, 1980-82 and 2022 correlated; 1987, 1990, 2000-02, 2008-09 and 2020 flight-to-quality). TWO GENUINE UPWARD ADJUSTMENTS, made by the two Wave-2 seats and correct: (i) the measure is G7 GDP-WEIGHTED and Japan+Germany+France+Italy at ~0.365 of the weight have structurally less room to rally 50 bp from low absolute nominal levels, with the JGB in a RISING regime at a 30-year high — so the weighted rally is mechanically damped even when the US rallies hard; (ii) the threshold is LENIENT in a way all three Wave-1 seats noted and none priced, since a merely 30 bp rally resolves YES and fast drawdowns (2020 took 33 days) often complete the yield rally after the trough. HOLDING IT DOWN, and this is the tension I will not hide: the MODAL cause of a -25% drawdown in my own distribution is an AI capex bust, which is a growth/deflation shock and therefore the shock MOST likely to produce a large bond rally and so to FAIL this test. That is why I stop at 0.42 rather than the 0.55 the plumbing-plus-measurement story alone would give.\nB = 0.80 x 0.42 x 1.03 (episode-count adjustment, ~1.4 expected episodes gives the bid more than one chance to fail) = 0.346.\n\nCORRELATION: strongly POSITIVE, rho ~ +0.50, unanimous across the panel and correct — a correlation-failure event is itself a MOVE spike that lifts the decade average, and one fiscal-dominance / levered-plumbing world generates both. 2022 is the type specimen, with a MOVE average near 120-130 and a failed bond rally in the same year. P(A|B) = 0.50 x 1.5 = 0.75.\n\nMULTIPLICATION: 0.346 x 0.75 = 0.260 -> 26.\n\nCOHERENCE CHECK WITH CQ4 AND CQ6, EXPLICIT. Upper bound from my own regime masses: a failed-bid, high-rate-vol world is a C-type world. P(CQ5) <= P(C) x P(event|C) + P(not-C) x P(event|not-C) = 0.27 x 0.75 + 0.73 x 0.15 = 0.203 + 0.110 = 0.31. My 26 sits inside that ceiling, so CQ5 is not claiming more disorder than my CQ6-C/D mass of 39 supports. On the other side, my CQ4 variance is what makes 26 rather than 16 defensible: a P50 of 2.2% real with a P90 of 3.4% is a 120 bp right-tail on a five-year AVERAGE, which is a high-rate-vol distribution by construction. A CQ5 of 16 alongside a CQ4 P90 of 3.4 would be incoherent.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.921Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the load-bearing plank of my entire payload. CQ2 falls from 44 to ~26 (conjunct A from 0.76 to ~0.48). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 27 to ~16, D from 12 to ~22, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix, probability mass shifts from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional row (-6.5 in C) toward my best (+6.0 in D), and cuts gold's weighted contribution hard. This is the cheapest single observation that falsifies me, and it is a measurement question, not a macro one.\",\"signpost\":\"The OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% y/y for four consecutive quarters, with Türkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5% — i.e. the five EM members do NOT hold the aggregate above the G7 core.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Refutes the MOVE level-scaling argument EMPIRICALLY rather than theoretically, which is the whole of my move above the Wave-1 centre. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~14 (conjunct A from 0.50 back to ~0.30). CQ4 P90 falls from 3.4 to ~3.0 on a smaller term-premium tail. CQ6-C falls ~3 points to B. Gold's C-column number falls ~1.5 pp and long nominal sovereigns improve ~1.0 pp in every column.\",\"signpost\":\"The ICE BofA MOVE Index three-year average over 2027-2029, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator, prints below 90 WHILE the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75% over the same three years.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the measured-versus-true-China channel that is worth ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 P50 and ~5 points of my CQ6-A, and is the main place I moved toward the red-team. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.9 to ~1.7 and P90 from 2.9 to ~2.7; CQ6-A falls from 18 to ~12 with the mass going to B (+5) and D (+1). China equities/onshore duration: the equity half is damaged in every column and the duration half improves. It would also partly vindicate the Bayesian seat's narrower P90 of 2.65 against mine.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr on the REALIZED/ESTIMATE line (not the forecast line) — i.e. the official series stops printing near its target.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies BOTH places I departed upward from the Wave-1 CQ4 centre: the non-US-convergence delta term (+0.20 pp) and my cut of the Reinhart-Sbrancia repression wedge from -0.20 to -0.14. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.2 to ~1.4 and P90 from 3.4 to ~2.9; P10 falls from 1.15 to ~0.6, restoring the repression tail that the 3:1 TIPS-anchor likelihood ratio currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~17 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~19. G7 long nominals, TIPS and cash are all mis-priced too low in my 2026-2030 buildout column.\",\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap) averages below 1.00% for twelve consecutive months, with the 10y JGB real yield still below 0.50%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the physical-energization cap that holds my CQ3 P90 at 2.9 and that makes power/grid (+7.5) and construction/trades (+6.5) my two best 2026-2030 rows. CQ3 P90 rises to ~3.2 and P50 to ~2.0; CQ6-A rises from 18 to ~24 with B falling. My upward correction to AI-Q4 (toward 0.73) reverses toward the locked 0.66, and realized AI-Q3 load returns toward the locked 998 TWh P50. In the matrix, power/grid buildout falls from 7.5 to ~4.5 and construction from 6.5 to ~4.0 as the scarcity rent evaporates, while the AI-complex column rises ~3 pp in A and B.\",\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks AND gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting — against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'growth'."}}